(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["cs-classes-2027-055"]={"v":1,"id":"cs-classes-2027-055","title":"DVRUO Classes CS Test Series | Databases | Topic Wise Test 5","sourceTitle":"DVRUO Classes CS Test Series | Databases | Topic Wise Test 5","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","code":"CS","provider":"DVRUO CLASSES","providerKey":"classes","series":"DVRUO Classes Test Series for GATE CSE 2027","seriesId":"cs-classes-2027","num":55,"category":"topicwise","year":"","format":"B","durationSec":2700,"info":{"orgName":"DVRUO Classes","batchName":"DVRUO Classes Test Series for GATE CSE 2027","subjectName":"Databases"},"source":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING/GO CLASSES/GO Classes Test Series for GATE CSE 2027/080_exam_1353_GO Classes CS Test Series Databases Topic Wise Test 5.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>A simple way to detect a state of deadlock is to construct a wait-for graph. One node is created in the graph for each transaction that is currently executing in the schedule. Whenever a transaction $\\text{T}_i$ is waiting to lock an item $\\text{X}$ that is currently locked by a transaction $\\text{T}_j,$ it creates a directed edge $(\\text{T}_i \\# \\text{T}_j).$ When $\\text{T}_j$ releases the lock(s) on the items that $\\text{T}_i$ was waiting for, the directed edge is dropped from the waiting-for graph.</p><p>Given the graph below, which is/are deadlock free?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" height=\"114\" width=\"179\" src=\"media/87/878c1406b93f32b906db.png\"><br>&nbsp;"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" height=\"117\" width=\"169\" src=\"media/ff/ffdcf8ae6dc3f2372391.png\"><br>&nbsp;"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" height=\"97\" width=\"223\" src=\"media/b3/b3658dc746b177c5dd88.png\"><br>&nbsp;"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" height=\"98\" width=\"229\" src=\"media/10/10770725daccce855d9d.png\"><br>&nbsp;"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 16)</small></h4><a name=\"385438\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">We have a state of deadlock if and only if the wait-for graph has a cycle.<br>\nIn graph $\\text{A},$ there is no cycle, so it is deadlock free.</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"390518\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">here every garph has cycle except the first one. so &nbsp;first is deadlock free.</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"493233\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">You can check for deadlock through cycle in graph or if any node h no out going edge then that also deadlock free so option A has no cycle or T2 has no outgoing edges so it is deadlock free<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/385437\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":2,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Serializability is the property that a (possibly interleaved) execution of a group of transactions has the same effect on the database and produces the same output as some serial execution of those transactions.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>The following schedule is serializable:<br>\n\t$\\mathrm{r} 1[\\mathrm{X}] \\rightarrow \\mathrm{w} 1[\\mathrm{X}] \\rightarrow \\mathrm{r} 2[\\mathrm{Y}] \\rightarrow \\mathrm{w} 2[\\mathrm{Y}] \\rightarrow \\mathrm{r} 2[\\mathrm{X}] \\rightarrow \\mathrm{w} 2[\\mathrm{X}] \\rightarrow \\mathrm{r} 1[\\mathrm{Z}] \\rightarrow \\mathrm{w} 1[\\mathrm{Z}] \\rightarrow \\mathrm{c} 1 \\rightarrow \\mathrm{c} 2$<br>\n\tHere in $\\mathrm{T} \\#[\\mathrm{A}],\\; \\text{T}$ represents operation(read/write)$, \\#$ represents transaction number$,\\; \\text{A}$ represents the item on which operation is being performed.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Strict two-phase locking $\\text{(2PL)}$ ensures that transactions never deadlock.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Strict two-phase locking $\\text{(2PL)}$ ensures conflict serializability.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 7)</small></h4><a name=\"385436\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Schedules that are always considered to be correct when concurrent transactions are executed. Such schedules are known as serializable schedules.<br>\n<br>\nThe concept of serializability of schedules is used to identify which schedules are correct when transaction executions have interleaving of their operations in the schedules.<br>\n<br>\nThe definition of serializable schedule is as follows: A schedule $\\mathrm{S}$ of $n$ transactions is serializable if it is equivalent to some serial schedule of the same $n$ transactions.<br>\n<br>\nSaying that a nonserial schedule $\\mathrm{S}$ is serializable is equivalent to saying that it is correct, because it is equivalent to a serial schedule, which is considered correct.<br>\n<br>\nStrict $\\text{2PL}$ ensures conflict serializability but is not deadlock free.<br>\n<br>\nPrecedence graph of option B schedule is Acyclic So, B is conflict serializable and equivalent to $\\mathrm{T} 0 \\rightarrow \\mathrm{T} 1$.</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"498802\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><img alt=\"ans for option c and d\" width=\"869\" height=\"1280\" src=\"media/fa/faf3f413b40bb471f70e.jpg\"></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>As for the Option A, it is the standard definition for serializability, and you can easily find out the conflict serializability by drawing the conflict graph.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/385435\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":3,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Assume by \"Serializability\", we mean \"Conflict Serializability\".<br>\nFor the given schedule, which of the following is TRUE?</p>\n\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" style=\"width:500px\">\n\t<tbody>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td>$\\text{T}_1$</td>\n\t\t\t<td>$\\text{T}_2$</td>\n\t\t\t<td>$\\text{T}_3$</td>\n\t\t\t<td>$\\text{T}_4$</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>Read(A)</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>Read(A)</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>Read(A)</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td>Write(B)</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>Write(A)</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>Read(B)</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>Write(B)</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t</tbody>\n</table>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>The above schedule is not serializable</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>The above schedule is equivalent to serial schedule $\\text{T}_3, \\text{T}_4, \\text{T}_1, \\text{T}_2$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>The above schedule is equivalent to serial schedule $\\text{T}_1, \\text{T}_4, \\text{T}_3, \\text{T}_2$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>The above schedule is equivalent to serial schedule $\\text{T}_2, \\text{T}_3, \\text{T}_1, \\text{T}_4$</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 9)</small></h4><a name=\"385434\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>The precedence graph is shown below :<br>\n&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"137\" src=\"media/bd/bd75fb125627592dffb5.png\" width=\"371\"></p>\n\n<p><br>\nFor this precedence graph, the topological orders are :</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>$\\text{T}_1, \\text{T}_4, \\text{T}_3, \\text{T}_2$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{T}_1, \\text{T}_3, \\text{T}_4, \\text{T}_2$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{T}_4, \\text{T}_1, \\text{T}_3, \\text{T}_2$</li>\n</ul></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"390520\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">in precendence graph r4 and t1 should shart first there is no cycle in precedence graph so it is conflict serializable</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/385433\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>To check for conflict serializability of any schedule, we draw its Precedence graph. In the precedence graph we have a node for each transaction $\\text{T}_i$ and An edge from $\\text{T}_i$ to $\\text{T}_j$ whenever an action in $\\text{T}_i$ conflicts with, and comes before an action in $\\text{T}_j.$</p>\n\n<p>For a conflict serializable schedule $\\text{S},$ which of the following is guaranteed to give a serial schedule that is conflict equivalent to $\\text{S},$ in the precedence Graph?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Depth-first order</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Topological order</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Breadth-first order</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 5)</small></h4><a name=\"385432\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>B is correct because DFS and BFS will lead to an order even if there exists a cycle in the precedence graph but topological sorting is only possible in the cycle-free graph. The cycle in the precedence graph indicates non-conflict serializability. Also even if there is no cycle in the precedence graph, DFS, BFS need not give an equivalent serial order.</p>\n\n<p>For example, take the following graph :</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>$\\text{T}_1 \\rightarrow \\text{T}_2, \\text{T}_3$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{T}_3 \\rightarrow \\text{T}_2$</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>If we start at $\\text{T}_1,$ then DFS,&nbsp;&nbsp;BFS may give $\\text{T}_1, \\text{T}_2, \\text{T}_3,$ which is Not equivalent serial order.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/385431\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following schedules:</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"313\" height=\"209\" src=\"media/3c/3c73355dd8961909de1c.png\"></p><div style=\"clear:both\"><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"311\" height=\"198\" src=\"media/42/42e33771918e24f0cd21.png\"></p></div><div style=\"clear:both\">Which of the above schedule(s) is/are conflict serializable?</div>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{S1}$ and $\\text{S2}$ only</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{S2}$ only</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{S3}$ and $\\text{S4}$ only</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None of these</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"385430\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><img alt=\"\" height=\"130\" src=\"media/a7/a7ae3aa29ed6d56bf39d.png\" width=\"523\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"390521\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">we just need to check that is there any conflit t1-&gt;t2 and t2-&gt;t1 if it is then its not conflict serializable &nbsp;else it is conflict serializable. we can also check it using precedence graph.<br>\n<br>\nhere in s2 we are getting conflict from T2 TO T1 but not from T1 &nbsp;to T2.</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/385429\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":6,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Suppose that transaction $\\mathrm{T} 1$ aborts after the following sequence of read write operations in the given schedule below :&nbsp;$$\\text{Write1(X);&nbsp; Write2(X);&nbsp; Write1(Y)};\\; \\operatorname{Read3}(\\mathrm{Y}) ;\\; \\operatorname{Read}4(\\mathrm{X}) ;\\; \\operatorname{Read}4(\\mathrm{Z})$$<br>\nWhich of the other transactions would need to be rolled back?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{T2}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{T3}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{T4}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 19)</small></h4><a name=\"385428\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">A Recoverable execution is one which allows commits only when all transactions that wrote data items read by the committing transaction have committed.<br>\nAn execution is recoverable if, for every transaction $\\mathrm{T}$ that commits, $\\text{T's}$ commit follows the commit of every transaction from which $\\mathrm{T}$ read.<br>\nSince, $\\text{T1}$ aborts, we see which transaction has read from $\\text{T1},$ that is only $\\text{T3}.$ So, $\\text{T3}$ should abort as well.</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 10)</small></h4><a name=\"416619\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>If one transaction failure causes multiple transactions to roll back, it is called as cascading roll back (or) cascading aborts.</p>\n\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" style=\"width:150px\">\n\t<thead>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<th scope=\"col\">T1</th>\n\t\t\t<th scope=\"col\">T2</th>\n\t\t\t<th scope=\"col\">T3</th>\n\t\t\t<th scope=\"col\">T4</th>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t</thead>\n\t<tbody>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td>w(x)</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>w(x)</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td>w(y)</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>R(y)</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>R(x)</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>&nbsp;</td>\n\t\t\t<td>R(z)</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Here, we can observe there is a dirty read conflicting operation between T1 →&nbsp;T3&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>So, rollback of T1 will result in rollback of T3.</p>\n\n<p>Hence, option B is correct</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"482515\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><img alt=\"\" width=\"1200\" height=\"489\" src=\"media/09/09987b06202faa96c69d.jpg\">.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"501828\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><div>HERE <strong>2 Blind writes exist</strong> in simple words</div><ul><li>For variable X : <strong>T2-&gt;T4</strong></li><li>For variable Y : <strong>T1-&gt;T3</strong></li></ul><div><strong><em>but only one blind write is considered here , as both of them are independent of each other here.</em></strong></div><div>&nbsp;</div><ul><li>T4 dependent on T2</li><li>T3 dependent on T1</li></ul><div>&nbsp;</div><p data-start=\"593\" data-end=\"718\">A blind write <strong data-start=\"607\" data-end=\"639\">does not create a dependency</strong>, because the later transaction does <strong data-start=\"676\" data-end=\"687\">not use</strong> the earlier uncommitted value.</p><p data-start=\"720\" data-end=\"730\">Therefore:</p><ul data-start=\"731\" data-end=\"849\"><li data-start=\"731\" data-end=\"788\"><p data-start=\"733\" data-end=\"788\"><strong data-start=\"733\" data-end=\"761\">T2 does not depend on T1</strong>, so it is not rolled back.</p></li><li data-start=\"789\" data-end=\"849\"><p data-start=\"791\" data-end=\"849\"><strong data-start=\"791\" data-end=\"822\">T4 depends on T2, not on T1</strong>, so it is not rolled back.</p></li></ul><h3 data-start=\"851\" data-end=\"885\">✔ Only T3 must be rolled back as its dependent on T1 (blind write)</h3><p data-start=\"851\" data-end=\"885\">hope you understand my explanation.&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/385427\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":7,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following concurrent execution of three transactions $\\text{T}_1, \\text{T}_2, \\text{T}_3 :$<br>\n$$\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|} \\hline&nbsp;\\text{T}_{1} &amp; \\text{T}_{2} &amp; \\text{T}_{3} \\\\\\hline w(x) &amp; &amp; \\\\&nbsp; &amp; r(y) &amp; \\\\&nbsp; &amp; &amp; w(z) \\\\ w(y) &amp; &amp; \\\\ &amp; r(z) &amp; \\\\&nbsp; &amp; &amp; w(z) \\\\\\hline&nbsp; \\end{array}$$</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>$w\\text{(P)}$ means Write operation on $\\text{“P\"}$</li>\n\t<li>$r\\text{(P)}$ means Read operation on $\\text{“P\"}$</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Which of the following is/are False about the given schedule?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>It is conflict serializable.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>It is view serializable.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>It can be produced by a Two Phase Lock $\\text{(2PL)}$ scheduler.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>It can be produced by a strict $2 \\mathrm{PL}$ scheduler.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 21)</small></h4><a name=\"385426\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>The precedence graph of the given schedule will be as follows :</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"130\" src=\"media/c4/c4b7aafcd8c6e5471517.png\" width=\"163\"></p>\n\n<p>It is not conflict serializable because of a cycle in the precedence graph.<br>\nIt is Not view serializable because $: \\mathrm{T}_2$ is reading $z$ which is written by $\\text{T}_3.$ Also final write of $z$ is being done by $\\text{T}_3.$ So, If we do $\\mathrm{T}_2$ before $\\mathrm{T}_3$ then $\\mathrm{T}_2$ will NOT be reading write of $z$ by $\\mathrm{T}_3$. If we do $\\mathrm{T}_3$ before $\\mathrm{T}_2$ then $\\mathrm{T}_2$ will be reading $a$ different write of $\\text{T}_3.$ So, there is a contradiction.<br>\n<br>\nNOTE that in view serializability, \"For each data item $\\mathrm{Q}$, if transaction Ti executes read $(\\mathrm{Q})$ in schedule $\\mathrm{S},$ and if that value was produced by a $\\text{write(Q)}$ operation executed by transaction $\\mathrm{T}_j,$ then the $\\text{read(Q)}$ operation of transaction $\\text{T}_i$ must, in schedule $\\text{S},$ also read the value of $\\text{Q}$ that was produced by the same $\\text{write(Q)}$ operation of transaction $\\mathrm{T}_j$.</p>\n\n<p>Any non-serializable schedule can not be $\\text{2PL}$ or strict $\\text{2PL.}$</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"445200\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Please eplain the view Serializability in this question...........</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/385425\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":8,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider a database schedule of two transactions $\\text{T1, T2}.\\;\\text{T1}$ first reads $\\text{A}$ and $\\text{B}$ and then writes $\\text{A}$ and $\\text{B}.\\; \\text{T2}$ reads and writes $\\text{A}$ then reads and writes $\\text{B}.$ Consider the following statements. Which of the following statements are correct?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Every concurrent/interleaved execution of these transactions will lead to a conflict serializable schedule.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Every concurrent/interleaved execution of these transactions will lead to a view serializable schedule.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>If a concurrent/interleaved execution of these transactions lead to a non-conflict serializable schedule then that schedule is also non-view serializable.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>If a concurrent/interleaved execution of these transactions lead to a non-view serializable schedule then that schedule is also non-conflict serializable.</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 31)</small></h4><a name=\"479797\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>So I actually spent a lot of time in understanding the question as well as understanding all the options of this question. I read the available answers in the discussion forum and i was able to understand them to some extent. I am writing this answer to provide a detailed explanation of the question and all the options so it would be helpful to somone who is confused with this question/answer.<br><br><strong>I will use the concept of Propositional Logic to solve this question.&nbsp;</strong>If you don't know Propositional Logic don't worry i will first explain the concept of propositional logic that will be used in the question.<br>&nbsp;</p><ol><li><h3><strong>The concept of Converse,Inverse and Contrapositive in Propositional Logic:</strong></h3></li></ol><div><strong>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</strong>Consider the Following conditional Statement(Y) : <strong>If weather is nice then I will wash the car.</strong></div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<em>Let W= <span style=\"color:#9b59b6;\">If weather is Nice</span> , C=<span style=\"color:#f1c40f;\"> I will wash the car&nbsp;</span></em></div><div><span style=\"color: #f1c40f;\"><i>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</i></span></div><div><i>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</i> &nbsp; &nbsp; So using normal Implication logic of IF_ then_:&nbsp; W -&gt; C&nbsp; &nbsp;or&nbsp; &nbsp;If W then C&nbsp; (This means if W happens then C happens)</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp;</div><p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<strong><u>Converse:</u>&nbsp;C-&gt;W&nbsp; or&nbsp; If C then W</strong>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<strong><u>Contrapositive:</u>&nbsp;~C-&gt;~W&nbsp; or&nbsp; If Not C then Not W</strong>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<u>Inverse</u>:&nbsp;~w-&gt;~C&nbsp; or&nbsp; If Not W then Not C</strong>&nbsp;</p><div><hr><p>&nbsp;</p></div><h3><strong>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; 2. Understanding and Solving the Question:</strong></h3><blockquote><div>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; <em>&nbsp; Consider a database schedule of two transactions T1, T2. T1 first reads A and B and then writes A and B. T2 reads&nbsp;</em></div><div>&nbsp;</div><div><em>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; and&nbsp; writes A&nbsp;then reads and writes B.</em></div></blockquote><div>&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; Using this statement from the question we should first make the Schedule, the schedule will be:</div><div>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img alt=\"\" width=\"357\" height=\"486\" src=\"media/59/59b098bb55196877fc9a.png\"></div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp;From the question we get this Schedule(S) having 2 transactions T1,T2 and this is a Serial Schedule because there is no&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp;Interleaved Execution of any operation.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div><strong>&nbsp; <u>Concept of Blind write:</u>&nbsp;</strong>Blind write means when a Transaction (Ti) just writes the data-item (X) without reading it's value.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp; &nbsp;Hence the old value of X was lost and transaction Ti Blindly overwrite the value of X.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp;In the above schedule we can see there is no&nbsp;<strong>Blind-write&nbsp;</strong>because T1 first reads the value of A,B before performing write</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp;operation and Transaction T2 also reads the value of A,B first.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div><hr><p><strong>&nbsp;Consider Option A:&nbsp;</strong><span style=\"color:#f39c12;\">&nbsp;</span><span style=\"color:#7f8c8d;\"><em>Every concurrent/interleaved execution of these transactions will lead to a conflict serializable schedule.</em></span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>For&nbsp;this option we have to take a counter example&nbsp; of a non-serial schedule which will not be conflict serializable. So you just</p><p>&nbsp;have to think of arranging the Operations of T1 and T2 in such a way that there is a cycle in the precedence graph. Also, don't</p><p>&nbsp;make the mistake of re-ordering the operations inside Transaction T1,T2 we should not do that. We have to create a non-serial</p><p>&nbsp;schedule with interleaved operations of T1, T2. Consider the following schedule:</p><p><br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e5/e5244040e9d8ab49d3ba.png\"></p><p>&nbsp;</p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;So here there is Conflicting operation b/w T1-&gt;T2 (A) and T2-&gt;T1(A) , hence a cycle. So option A is false because we were<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; able to make a non-serial schedule which is not conflict-serializable.</p><hr><p><strong>Now before moving to other options we need to derive some conclusions using the Propositional Logic, as it will help</strong></p><p><strong>&nbsp;us to solve the other options.</strong></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><ul><li>From our theory Knowledge we know: If a Schedule is&nbsp;<strong>Conflict serializable&nbsp;then it it View serializable&nbsp;as well.</strong>&nbsp;</li></ul><p>&nbsp;<em>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; Let CS= If a Schedule is Conflict&nbsp;serializable&nbsp;,&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;VS= Then it is view&nbsp;serializable.</em></p><p><em>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; So,&nbsp; &nbsp; CS-&gt; VS&nbsp; (If a schedule is CS then it will be VS, Always TRUE)</em></p><p><i>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</i>Now we know there is no Blind write in the question and we also know that if Blind write doesn't exist in a schedule then&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;it cannot be<strong> view-serializable.&nbsp;</strong>Consider the following Flow-chart:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<br><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a2/a20b3b490cd8b8297f85.png\">&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p><i>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</i><br><strong>So we can make some Final Conclusions based on the information we have.&nbsp;</strong></p><p>&nbsp;</p><ul><li><b>If a schedule is CS then it will definitely be VS.</b><hr></li><li><b>If a Schedule is VS then it will definitely be CS. (Because Blind write&nbsp;doesn't exist so&nbsp;a Schedule which is not Conflict serializable but view-serializable&nbsp;will not exist cuz we don't have blind write).</b><hr>&nbsp;<p><strong>Consider Option B:&nbsp;<span style=\"color:#7f8c8d;\">Every concurrent/interleaved execution of these transactions will lead to a view serializable schedule.</span></strong>&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;</p></li></ul><div>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From option A we concluded that a non-serial schedule which is not conflict serializable can exist that means the same schedule will not be View serializable also cuz there is not blind write&nbsp; in the schedule.</div><div><br>Y: Cs-&gt;VS</div></div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>~Cs -&gt; ~VS (inverse-concept of Propositional Logic).&nbsp;</div><div>This means if a schedule is not CS then it is not VS, hence making <strong>Option B false.</strong></div><div>&nbsp;</div><div><b><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">***Please Note:&nbsp; Under normal conditions i.e if a Schedule has Blind write then this concept of inverse may/may not hold because there can exist a Schedule which is not CS but can be VS (due to Blind write).***</span></b></div><div>&nbsp;</div><div><hr></div><p>&nbsp;<strong>Consider Option C: <span style=\"color:#7f8c8d;\">If a concurrent/interleaved execution of these transactions lead to a non-conflict serializable schedule then that schedule is also non-view serializable.</span></strong></p><p>This option says that if We get a non-serial schedule which is not CS then that schedule is also not VS.&nbsp;<strong>This is True.</strong></p><div><div>Y: Cs-&gt;VS</div></div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>~Cs -&gt; ~VS (inverse-concept of Propositional Logic).&nbsp;</div><div>This means if a schedule is not CS then it is not VS</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div><strong>Hence Option C is True.</strong></div><div>&nbsp;</div><hr><p>&nbsp;</p><div><strong>Consider Option D:<span style=\"color:#7f8c8d;\">&nbsp;If a concurrent/interleaved execution of these transactions lead to a non-view serializable schedule that schedule is also non-conflict serializable.</span></strong><span style=\"color:#7f8c8d;\">&nbsp;</span> &nbsp;&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>This option says that if a Non-serial schedule is not VS then it will not be CS also.&nbsp;<strong>This is also TRUE.</strong></div><div>&nbsp;</div><div><div><div>Y: Cs-&gt;VS</div></div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>~VS-&gt; ~CS (Contrapositive-concept of Propositional Logic).&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>This means if a schedule is not VS then it is not CS. Under normal conditions also this will hold true, consider the flow-chart above. If a schedule is not VS then definitely it cannot be CS.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>So&nbsp;<strong>Option D is also TRUE.</strong></div><div>&nbsp;</div><div><hr><strong>PS:&nbsp;</strong>I know this a bit long explanation but i hope you are able to understand the whole question as well as the options. Because Deepak sir always says students usually only understand the correct options but don't bother to understand why the other options were not correct. So my aim was help you understand everything in this question so in future if same type of question arises you should be able to solve it.&nbsp;<br><br>If someone finds any mistake in my logic, let me know i will correct it.😀&nbsp;</div></div><div>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div><br><br>&nbsp;</div></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 12)</small></h4><a name=\"385424\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Given below is an example schedule that is not (conflict) serializable :<br>\n$$\\mathrm{R} 1[\\mathrm{~A}] \\rightarrow \\mathrm{R} 1[\\mathrm{~B}] \\rightarrow \\mathrm{R} 2[\\mathrm{~A}] \\rightarrow \\mathrm{W} 2[\\mathrm{~A}] \\rightarrow \\mathrm{R} 2[\\mathrm{~B}] \\rightarrow \\mathrm{W} 1[\\mathrm{~A}] $$$$ \\rightarrow \\mathrm{W} 1[\\mathrm{~B}] \\rightarrow \\mathrm{W} 2[\\mathrm{~B}]\\rightarrow \\mathrm{C} 1 \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C} 2$$<br>\nA schedule is (conflict) serializable if it contains the same transactions and operations as a serial schedule and the order of all conflicting operations (read/writes to the same objects by different transactions) is also the same. In the above schedule, $\\text{T1}$ reads $\\text{A}$ before $\\text{T2}$ writes $\\mathrm{A}.$ However, $\\text{T1}$ writes $\\text{A}$ after $\\text{T2}$ reads and writes it.<br>\n<br>\nThe schedule is thus clearly not serializable. Additionally, according to the above schedule, the final content of object $\\mathrm{A}$ is written by $\\mathrm{T} 1$ and the final content of object $\\text{B}$ is written by $\\text{T2}.$ Such a result is not possible in any serial execution, where transactions execute one after the other in sequence. Not view serializable implies not conflict serializable.</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 8)</small></h4><a name=\"390525\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">here we can get &nbsp;schedules which are not conflict serializability<br>\n<br>\nand in that case there is no biind write so they will also be non view serializable<br>\n<br>\nso option c is correct.<br>\n<br>\nif it is not view serializable then its guaranteed that it is not conflict serializable</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"534018\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>in the given quetion i you oberve that they was not blind writing so,in that case conflict = view&nbsp;</p><p>option c:&nbsp;</p><ol><li>If a concurrent/interleaved execution of these transactions lead to a non-conflict serializable schedule then that schedule is also non-view serializable.</li></ol><div>if the give was not a non-conflict so that it was definly not the non-view&nbsp;</div><div>option D:<ol><li>If a concurrent/interleaved execution of these transactions lead to a non-view serializable schedule then that schedule is also non-conflict serializable.</li></ol><section id=\"section-post-tags\"><nav aria-label=\"Tags:\"><ul></ul></nav></section><section>in the same as the option c it was not the view so that it was also the non-conflict</section></div></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/385423\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following schedule $\\text{S :}$</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"362\" src=\"media/32/324f63c3aa85dd113d85.png\" width=\"322\"></p>\n\n<p><br>\nThe time-stamps of $\\text{T1, T2}$ are $1,2$ respectively then which of the following is/are Incorrect?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{S}$ is possible under basic Timestamp Ordering (T/O) protocol.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{S}$ is possible under THOMAS WRITE RULE Timestamp Ordering (T/O) protocol.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{S}$ is conflict serializable.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\text{S}$ is view serializable.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"385422\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Not possible under basic T/O.</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"358\" src=\"media/bf/bfb41ac836908807ed41.png\" width=\"633\"></p>\n\n<p>Not allowed under Thomas write the rule as well because of $\\text{R(A)}$ of $\\text{T1}.$</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/385421\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":10,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following schedule $\\text{S :}$<br>\n$$\\text{start1; start2; r1(A); r2(B); w2(A); w1(B);}$$<br>\n$\\text{“start #\"}$ means beginning of transaction $\\#,$ then which of the following is/are Incorrect?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{S}$ is possible under basic Timestamp Ordering (T/O) protocol.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{S}$ is possible under THOMAS WRITE RULE Timestamp Ordering (T/O) protocol.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{S}$ is conflict serializable.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{S}$ is view serializable.</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B, C, D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 18)</small></h4><a name=\"414777\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>In the precedence graph \\(G\\) corresponding to the schedule \\(S\\) (where we denote transaction \\(\\#1\\) as \\(T_1\\), and transaction \\(\\#2\\) as \\(T_2\\)), we have both the edges \\(T_1 \\rightarrow T_2\\) (as \\(r_1(A)\\) conflicts with \\(w_2(A)\\)), and \\(T_2 \\rightarrow T_1\\) (as \\(r_2(B)\\) conflicts with \\(w_1(B)\\)). Thus, there is cycle in the graph \\(G\\), and so, the schedule \\(S\\) is not conflict-serializable. So, option the \\((C)\\) is false.</p>\n\n<p>In the schedule \\(S\\), \\(r_1(A)\\) and \\(r_2(B)\\) read in the original values of the data items \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), respectively, in the database, whereas, in any serial schedule, at least one of those <em>reads</em> read in the modified value of the concerned data item, thus causing a violation of the first requirement for view serializability. Therefore, the schedule \\(S\\) is not view-serializable. So, option the \\((D)\\) is false.</p>\n\n<p>The basic timestamp ordering protocol produces conflict-serializable schedules, whereas the timestamp ordering protocol with Thomas write rule produces conflict-serializable schedules along with a <em>few&nbsp;</em>view-serializable schedules. If a schedule itself isn’t view-serializable, then none of these variants of the timestamp ordering protocol helps. So, both the options \\((A)\\) and \\((B)\\) are false.</p>\n\n<p>Correct Answer : \\((A), (B), (C), (D)\\)</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 9)</small></h4><a name=\"385420\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Answer: A,B,C,D.</p>\n\n<p>Each transaction $\\mathrm{Ti}$ is issued a timestamp $\\mathrm{TS}(\\mathrm{Ti}$ ) when it enters the system.<br>\nIf an old transaction $\\mathrm{Ti}$ has timestamp $\\mathrm{TS}(\\mathrm{Ti})$, a new transaction $\\mathrm{Tj}$ has timestamp $\\mathrm{TS}(\\mathrm{Tj})$ where $\\mathrm{TS}(\\mathrm{Ti})&lt;\\mathrm{TS}(\\mathrm{Tj})$.</p>\n\n<p>So, Assume $\\text{TS(T1) = 1, TS(T2) = 2}$</p>\n\n<p>When $\\text{T1}$ performs $\\mathrm{W}(\\mathrm{B}), \\mathrm{RTS}(\\mathrm{B})=2$, So, $\\mathrm{W_1}(\\mathrm{B})$ is rejected by basic $\\mathrm{T} / \\mathrm{O}$ and $\\mathrm{T} 1$ is rollback. Thomas write rule also doesn’t allow&nbsp;this schedule.<br>\n<br>\n<strong>Thomas write rule : </strong>When $\\text{Ti}$ attempts to write data item $\\text{Q},$ if $\\mathrm{TS}(\\mathrm{Ti})&lt;\\mathrm{W}-$timestamp $(\\mathrm{Q})$, then $\\text{Ti}$ is attempting to write an obsolete value of $\\text{{Q}}$. Hence, rather than rolling back $\\text{Ti}$ as the timestamp ordering protocol would have done, this write operation can be ignored. Otherwise, the protocol is unchanged.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/385419\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":11,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following schedule:<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{gathered}<br>\n\\text{S = (2, W, x),(1, W, z),(1, W, x), C1,(2, W, y),(2, R, y)}<br>\n\\text{(3, W, z),(3, W, m), C3,(2, R, m), C2}.<br>\n\\end{gathered}<br>\n$$<br>\nWhere $\\text{Ci}$ means commit of $\\text{i}^{\\text{th}}$ transaction. Triple $\\text{(i, W / R, x)}$ means Transaction $\\text{i}$ writes/reads item $\\text{x}.$<br>\nWhich of the following statements is/are true for $\\text{S}?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{S}$ is conflict serializable.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{S}$ is view serializable but not conflict serializable.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{S}$ is not view serializable.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 21)</small></h4><a name=\"385418\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Check for View serializability :</strong><br>\n<br>\nTransaction $2$ is reading item $m$ which is written by trans $3,$ hence, in the view equivalent serial schedule, Trans $2$ must appear after (not necessarily immediately) Trans $3.\\; (3 \\rightarrow 2)$<br>\n<br>\nTransaction $1$ is doing the final write of item $x$ which is also written by trans $2,$ hence, in the view equivalent serial schedule, Trans $1$ must appear after(not necessarily immediately) Trans $2. \\;(2\\rightarrow 1)$<br>\n<br>\nTransaction $3$ is doing the final write of item $z$ which is also written by trans $1,$ hence, in the view equivalent serial schedule, Trans $3$ must appear after(not necessarily immediately) Trans $1. \\;(1\\rightarrow 3)$<br>\n<br>\nBut now we have a contradiction. &nbsp;$3 \\rightarrow 2 \\rightarrow 1$ But also $1 \\rightarrow 3.$</p>\n\n<p>Hence, Not view serializable.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 5)</small></h4><a name=\"482518\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Answer = C (Not view)</strong></p><p><strong><img alt=\"\" width=\"1153\" height=\"1572\" src=\"media/22/22e971d2694d6fc1e676.jpg\"></strong></p><p>&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/385417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":12,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following schedule :<br>\n$$\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}<br>\n\\hline \\mathrm{T}_1 &amp; \\mathrm{~T}_2 &amp; \\mathrm{~T}_3 &amp; \\mathrm{~T}_4 \\\\<br>\n\\hline \\mathrm{W}(\\mathrm{a}) &amp; &amp; &amp; \\\\<br>\n\\hline &amp; \\mathrm{R}(\\mathrm{a}) &amp; &amp; \\\\<br>\n\\hline &amp; &amp; \\mathrm{W}(\\mathrm{a}) &amp; \\\\&nbsp;<br>\n\\hline \\mathrm{W}(\\mathrm{a}) &amp; &amp; &amp; \\\\<br>\n\\hline &amp; &amp; &amp; \\mathrm{W}(\\mathrm{a}) \\\\<br>\n\\hline \\end{array}$$<br>\nWhich of the following is/are true for this schedule?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>It is conflict serializable but not view serializable.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>It is view serializable but not conflict serializable.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>It is neither conflict nor view serializable.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>It is both conflict and view serializable.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 20)</small></h4><a name=\"385416\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>The precedence graph is as following :<br>\n&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"149\" src=\"media/9c/9c1c8a5f746995fcaf77.png\" width=\"178\"></p>\n\n<p><br>\nIt is not conflict serializable because of a cycle in the precedence graph.<br>\n<br>\nIt is Not view serializable because :<br>\n$\\mathrm{T}_2$ is reading \"$a$\" which is written by $\\text{T}_1.$ So, If we do $\\text{T}_2$ before $\\text{T}_1$ then $\\text{T}_2$ will NOT be reading write of a by $\\text{T}_1.$ If we do $\\mathrm{T}_1$ before $\\mathrm{T}_2$ then $\\mathrm{T}_2$ will be reading a different write of $\\text{T}_1.$ So, there is a contradiction.<br>\n<br>\nNOTE that in view serializability,<br>\n\"For each data item $\\text{Q},$ if transaction $\\text{T}_i$ executes $\\text{read(Q)}$ in schedule $\\mathrm{S},$ and if that value was produced by a $\\text{write(Q)}$ operation executed by transaction $\\mathrm{T}_j$, then the $\\text{read(Q)}$ operation of transaction $\\text{T}_i$ must, in schedule $\\text{S},$ also read the value of $\\text{Q}$ that was produced by the same $\\text{write(Q)}$ operation of transaction $\\mathrm{T}_j$.<br>\n<br>\nNot only view from same transaction matters BUT also from SAME Write operation of same transaction matters.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"454388\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Checking for view serializability<br>\n<br>\nFinal write in a---- &nbsp;T4<br>\n<br>\nSo possible serial schedules that CAN be view equivalent to given serial schedules are<br>\n<br>\nT1-T2-T3-T4<br>\n<br>\nT1-T3-T2-T4<br>\n<br>\nIN T1-T2-T3-T4 we are shifting W(a) operation of T1 above R(a) T2 which will be conflicting swap hence we can't do that .<br>\n<br>\nIn T1-T3-T2-T4 we are shifting W(a) operation of T3 above R(a) of T2 which will be again a conflicting swap hence<br>\n<br>\nWe cant have any serial schedules that is view equivalent to given schedule so not view serializalble.<br>\n<br>\nADDITIONAL INFO - NOTE THAT THE SCHEDULE IS NOT CONFLICT SERIALIZABLE DUE TO PRESENCE OF CYCLE NOR VIEW SERIALIZALBLE BUT IS DEFINATELY SERIALIZALBLE.<br>\n<br>\nTHIS IS BECAUSE T4 IS THE ONE WHO IS PERFORMING FINAL WRITE WITHOUT READING ANY VALUE ie BLIND WRITE , SO NO MATTER WHAT CONFLICTS ARE WITH T1,T2,T3 BUT T4 WILL ALWAYS UPDATE FINAL VALUE HENCE ITS RESULT EQUIVALENT SO SERIALIZALBLE.<br>\n<br>\nITS A MAJOR MISCONCEPTIONS THAT IF A SCHEDULE IS NEITHER VIEW OR CONFLICT SERIAZIBILE THEN IT CANT BE SERIALIZALBLE BUT ITS NOT TRUE AS MENTIONED SCHEDULE IS SERIALIZALBLE HAVING NO CONFLICT AND VIEW EQUIVALENT SCHEDULES.<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/385415\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":13,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following schedule $\\mathrm{S}$ :<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{array}{llllllll}<br>\nT_1: &amp; &amp; &amp; r_1(A) &amp; &amp; w_1(B) &amp; &amp; \\\\<br>\nT_2: &amp; r_2(B) &amp; w_2(A) &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; w_2(B) &amp; \\\\<br>\nT_3: &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; r_3(A) &amp; &amp; &amp; w_3(B) \\\\<br>\n\\end{array}<br>\n$$<br>\nWhich of the following is/are true for this schedule?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>It is conflict serializable but not view serializable.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>It is view serializable but not conflict serializable.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>It is neither conflict nor view serializable.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>It is both conflict and view serializable.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 6)</small></h4><a name=\"385414\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>The precedence graph is as following :<br>\n&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"134\" src=\"media/d5/d5851a7dff97866b86d8.png\" width=\"148\"></p>\n\n<p><br>\n<strong>For view serializability :</strong><br>\n<br>\nImagine that there is a hypothetical transaction $\\text{T}_0$ that wrote initial values for each database element read by any transaction in the schedules, and another hypothetical transaction $\\text{T}_f$ that reads every element written by one or more transactions after each schedule ends.<br>\n<br>\nNow, In $\\text{S},$ both $\\text{T}_1$ and $\\text{T}_2$ write values of $\\text{B}$ that are lost; only the value of $\\text{B}$ written by $\\text{T}_3$ survives to the end of the schedule &nbsp;and is “read\" by the hypothetical transaction $\\text{T}_f .\\; \\text{S}$ is not conflict-serializable. To see why, first note that $\\text{T}_2$ writes $\\text{A}$ before $\\text{T}1$ reads $\\text{A},$ so $\\text{T}_2$ must precede $\\text{T}_1$ in a hypothetical conflict-equivalent serial schedule. The fact that the action $w_1\\text{(B)}$ precedes $w_2\\text{(B)}$ also forces $\\text{T}_1$ to precede $\\text{T}_2$ in any conflict-equivalent serial schedule. Yet neither $w_1\\text{(B)}$ nor $w_2\\text{(B)}$ has any long-term affect on the database. It is these sorts of irrelevant writes that view-serializability is able to ignore, when determining the true constraints on an equivalent serial schedule.<br>\n<br>\nMore precisely, let us consider the sources of all the reads in $\\text{S:}$</p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li>The source of $r_2\\text{(B)}$ is $\\text{T}_0,$ since there is no prior write of $\\text{B}$ in $\\text{S}.$</li>\n\t<li>The source of $r_1\\text{(A)}$ is $\\text{T}_2,$ since $\\text{T}_2$ most recently wrote A before the read.</li>\n\t<li>Likewise, the source of $r_3\\text{(A)}$ is $\\text{T}_2.$</li>\n\t<li>The source of the hypothetical read of $\\text{A}$ by $\\text{T}_f$ is $\\text{T}_2.$</li>\n\t<li>The source of the hypothetical read of $\\text{B}$ by $\\text{T}_f$ is $\\text{T}_3,$ the last writer of $\\text{B}.$</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Of course, $\\text{T}_0$ appears before all real transactions in any schedule, and $\\text{T}_f$ appears after all transactions. If we order the real transactions $(\\text{T}_2; \\text{T}_1; \\text{T}_3),$ then the sources of all reads are the same as in schedule $\\text{S}.$ That is, $\\text{T}_2$ reads $\\text{B},$ and surely $\\text{T}_0$ is the previous “writer.\" $\\text{T}_1$ reads $\\text{A},$ but $\\text{T}_2$ already wrote $\\text{A},$ so the source of $r_1\\text{(A)}$ is $\\text{T}2,$ as in $\\text{S. T}_3$ also reads $\\text{A},$ but since the prior $\\text{T}_2$ wrote $\\text{A},$ that is the source of $r_3\\text{(A)},$ as in $\\text{S}.$ Finally, the hypothetical $\\text{T}_f$ reads $A$ and $B,$ but the last writers of $\\text{A}$ and $\\text{B}$ in the schedule $(\\text{T}_2; \\text{T}_1; \\text{T}_3)$ are $\\text{T}_2$ and $\\text{T}_3$ respectively, also as in $\\text{S}.$ We conclude that $\\text{S}$ is a view-serializable schedule, and the schedule represented by the order $\\text{(T}_2; \\text{T}_1; \\text{T}_3)$ is a view-equivalent schedule.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/385413\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":14,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let transactions $\\text{T1, T2}$ and $\\text{T3}$ be defined to perform the following operations :</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>$\\text{T1} :$ Add one to $\\text{A}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{T2} :$ Double $\\text{A}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{T3} :$ Display $\\text{A}$ on the screen and then set it to $1$ ie. $\\text{A} = 1.$</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>(where $\\text{A}$ is some item in the database)<br>\nSuppose transactions $\\text{T1, T2}$ and $\\text{T3}$ are allowed to execute concurrently. If $\\text{A}$ has initial value zero, report the possible values of final value of $\\text{A}$ and the displayed value of $\\text{A}.$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$1,2$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$2,1$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$3,0$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$4,0$</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 11)</small></h4><a name=\"429257\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" style=\"width: 500px; margin: auto; border-spacing: 1px;\"><caption>Total 6 Order Possible</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Order Of Execution</th><th scope=\"col\">(Final Value, Display Value)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"text-align: center;\">T1, T2, T3</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">(1,2)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: center;\">T1, T3, T2</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">(2,1)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: center;\">T2, T3, T1</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">(2,0)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: center;\">T2, T1, T3</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">(1,1)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: center;\">T3, T2, T1</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">(3,0)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: center;\">T3, T1, T2</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">(4,0)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>&nbsp;</p><div>Hence, Correct Options are&nbsp;<strong>A,B,C,D.</strong></div></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"385412\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">$\\text{A- T1, T2, T3, B - T1, T3, T2, D- T3, T2, T1, C - T3, T1, T2}$</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"439413\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">T1: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;T2: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;T3:<br>\nR(A) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;R(A) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;R(A)<br>\nA=A+1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A=A*2 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Display(A)<br>\nW(A) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;W(A) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;W(A) \\\\ it is dirty write as A=1<br>\n<br>\noption a) &nbsp;to print 1,2<br>\nschedule: &nbsp;T1 T2 T3<br>\n<br>\noption b) &nbsp;to print 2,1<br>\nschedule: T1 T3 T2<br>\n<br>\noption c) to print 3,0<br>\nschedule: T3 T2 T1<br>\n<br>\noption d) to print 4,0<br>\nschedule: T3 T1 T2</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/385411\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":15,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>In the following, when a transaction $\\mathrm{T}$ performs an operation $x=x+a$, then $\\mathrm{T}$ reads $x$ into some register, adds a to the register content then stores register content into $x$.</p>\n\n<p>A transaction schedule is serializable if its effect is equivalent to that of some serial schedule. Consider a bookkeeping operation consisting of two transactions $-\\text{T}_1$ and $\\text{T}_2-$ that are required to keep the sum $\\text{A+B+C}$ unchanged. Which of the following pairs of transactions will always result in a serializable schedule?</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"427\" src=\"media/de/dec15fd5ee9efa9c22f3.png\" width=\"381\"></p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>I only</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>II only</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>III only</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>I and II</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 28)</small></h4><a name=\"389437\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">This can be visualized as<br>\n<br>\nData item B is shared between two transactions, Put B in the Critical Section and access it using the Lock mechanism.<br>\n<br>\nOnly II satisfies this condition.<br>\n<br>\nHence Option B</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 17)</small></h4><a name=\"385410\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Assume initially, $\\text{A = 10, B = 20, C = 30.}$<br>\n$\\text{A + B + C = 60}$<br>\nIn I, assume we execute statement $1,2,3$ of $\\text{T}_1.$ Then $\\text{T}_1$ read $\\text{B}$ value $20$ and preempt. Now $\\text{T}_2$ executes completely. Then $\\text{T}_1$ writes $30$ in $\\text{B}.$ Now, at the end, $\\text{A + B + C} = 80.$ So, $\\text{T}_1$ not correct.<br>\nIn III, assume we execute statement $1,2,3$ of $\\text{T}_1.$ Then $\\text{T}_1$ read $\\text{B}$ value $20$ and preempt. Now $\\text{T}_2$ executes completely. Then $\\text{T}_1$ writes $30$ in $\\text{B}.$ Now, at the end, $\\text{A + B + C} = 80.$ So, $\\text{T}_1$ not correct.<br>\nII is correct.</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"478771\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>I have broken down the first schedule for people who didnt understand the original answer. You can do rest 2 schedules in similar way on a paper and answer will be option(b).</p><p><img alt=\"\" width=\"364\" height=\"483\" src=\"media/3a/3a499974896298abc38d.jpg\"></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/385409\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true}],"sectionsMeta":[{"name":"Technical","marks":25.0,"instructions":null}],"math":true,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"Technical","start":0,"count":15}],"stats":{"questions":15,"maxMarks":9,"types":{"MSQ":8,"MCQ":7},"withoutKey":10}};
